closing:
For now, the best working approach I've found is to utilize the Kafka Offsets from the output records.

The approach can be summarized as:


Perform all the logic that you want to do and don't worry about multiple records for the same key.
Write the results into an intermediate topic with a very minimal retention (1 hour etc.)
Read the intermediate topics using a Processor and within the Processor, enrich the message with the Kafka Offset using context.offset().
Write the messages out to the output topic.


Now, your output topic contains multiple messages for the same key but each with a different offset.

During query time now you can select the max offsets for each key using a subquery.

An example TransformerSupplier can be seen below

/**
 * @param <K> key type
 * @param <V> value type
 */
public class OutputTransformSupplier<K, V> implements TransformerSupplier<K, V, KeyValue<String, String>> {
  @Override
  public Transformer<K, V, KeyValue<String, String>> get() {
    return new OutputTransformer<>();
  }

  private class OutputTransformer<K, V> implements Transformer<K, V, KeyValue<String, String>> {
    private ProcessorContext context;

    @Override
    public void init(ProcessorContext context) {
      this.context = context;
    }

    /**
     * @param key   the key for the record
     * @param value the value for the record
     */
    @Override
    public KeyValue<String, String> transform(K key, V value) {
      if (value != null) {
        value.setKafkaOffset(context.offset());
      }
      return new KeyValue<>(key, value);
    }

    @Override
    public KeyValue<String, String> punctuate(long timestamp) {
      return null;
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
      // nothing to close
    }
  }
}

