Using a GlobalKTable means that the Kafka topic will be fully replicated to each KafkaStreams that belongs to your application.id, either in memory or on disk (i.e. RocksDB) depending on how you materialise your StateStore.
A GlobalKTable is used to perfom a join with a KStream using a join-key that can be different from the key of the input streamw without having to repartition the input topics.
Typically, GlobalKTable are used to materialize a topic containing reference data or data that slowly changing over time.
One drawback of GlobalKTable is that KafkaStreams only use a single global thread to manage all global tables. This is why the initialization of multiple GlobalKTable can take a while, a few seconds to hours.
To optimize initialization you can override/increased those settings:

```
// for global consumer (default values).
streamsConfig.put(StreamsConfig.globalConsumerPrefix(ConsumerConfig.MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG), 1000);
streamsConfig.put(StreamsConfig.globalConsumerPrefix(ConsumerConfig.FETCH_MAX_BYTES_CONFIG), 52428800);
streamsConfig.put(StreamsConfig.globalConsumerPrefix(ConsumerConfig.MAX_PARTITION_FETCH_BYTES_CONFIG), 1048576);
streamsConfig.put(StreamsConfig.globalConsumerPrefix(ConsumerConfig.RECEIVE_BUFFER_CONFIG), 65536);

```

Moreover, the input topics for the GlobalKTable should be compacted (i.e. cleanup.policy=compact). Also, you may try to reduce the segment size of your topic (for example to 100MB) to increase the frequency of segment rotation and thus to have a more aggressive compaction.

